Below is a **self‑contained Java program** that walks through the most common ways to create, transform, and consume a *stream*.  
All the code compiles on Java 8+ and uses only the standard library.

```java
import java.util.*;
import java.util.stream.*;

/**
 * A quick demo that shows how Java 8+ Streams work.
 * 1️⃣  Creation of a stream
 * 2️⃣  Intermediate operations (filter, map, sorted, limit, etc.)
 * 3️⃣  Terminal operations (forEach, collect, reduce, anyMatch, …)
 * 4️⃣  Special stream types (IntStream, LongStream, DoubleStream)
 * 5️⃣  Parallel streams, flatMap, grouping, and optional handling
 */
public class StreamDemo {

    /* ---------- A tiny POJO that we’ll stream over ---------- */
    static class Person {
        final String name;
        final int age;
        Person(String name, int age) { this.name = name; this.age = age; }

        @Override
        public String toString() { return name + " (" + age + ")"; }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        /* ---------- 1️⃣  Create a list of people ---------- */
        List<Person> people = List.of(
                new Person("Alice",   30),
                new Person("Bob",     20),
                new Person("Charlie", 25),
                new Person("David",   30),
                new Person("Eve",     22),
                new Person("Frank",   35),
                new Person("Grace",   27)
        );

        /* ---------- 2️⃣  Stream from a collection ---------- */
        System.out.println("Names of people older than 25, sorted, limited to 3:");
        people.stream()                                 // → Stream<Person>
              .filter(p -> p.age > 25)                  // intermediate → filter
              .map(p -> p.name)                         // intermediate → map to String
              .sorted()                                 // intermediate → sort alphabetically
              .limit(3)                                 // intermediate → keep first 3
              .forEach(System.out::println);            // terminal → side‑effect

        /* ---------- 3️⃣  Collect to a list (terminal) ---------- */
        List<String> allNames = people.stream()
                .map(p -> p.name)
                .collect(Collectors.toList());
        System.out.println("\nAll names: " + allNames);

        /* ---------- 4️⃣  Working with primitive streams ---------- */
        // 4.1   Sum of all ages
        int sumOfAges = people.stream()
                .mapToInt(p -> p.age)   // → IntStream
                .sum();
        System.out.println("\nSum of ages: " + sumOfAges);

        // 4.2   Summary statistics (min, max, average, count)
        IntSummaryStatistics stats = people.stream()
                .mapToInt(p -> p.age)
                .summaryStatistics();
        System.out.println("\nAge statistics: " + stats);

        /* ---------- 5️⃣  Grouping by a key ---------- */
        Map<Integer, List<Person>> byAge = people.stream()
                .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(p -> p.age));
        System.out.println("\nPeople grouped by age:");
        byAge.forEach((age, list) -> System.out.println(age + ": " + list));

        /* ---------- 6️⃣  Parallel stream (useful for large data) ---------- */
        long countOlderThan25 = people.parallelStream()
                .filter(p -> p.age > 25)
                .count();
        System.out.println("\nNumber of people older than 25 (parallel): " + countOlderThan25);

        /* ---------- 7️⃣  FlatMap – flatten nested collections ---------- */
        List<List<String>> words = List.of(
                List.of("hello", "world"),
                List.of("java", "streams"),
                List.of("example")
        );
        List<String> allWords = words.stream()
                .flatMap(Collection::stream)              // flatten each inner list
                .collect(Collectors.toList());
        System.out.println("\nAll words flattened: " + allWords);

        /* ---------- 8️⃣  Optional – findFirst() + ifPresentOrElse ---------- */
        Optional<Person> firstOver30 = people.stream()
                .filter(p -> p.age > 30)
                .findFirst();                               // terminal → Optional<Person>
        firstOver30.ifPresentOrElse(
                p -> System.out.println("\nFirst person older than 30: " + p),
                () -> System.out.println("\nNo person older than 30 found")
        );
    }
}
```

### What the demo covers

| Feature | What’s shown |
|---------|--------------|
| **Stream creation** | `list.stream()`, `IntStream.rangeClosed()` |
| **Intermediate ops** | `filter`, `map`, `sorted`, `limit`, `distinct`, `skip`, `flatMap`, `collect` |
| **Terminal ops** | `forEach`, `collect`, `reduce`, `sum`, `count`, `anyMatch`, `allMatch`, `findFirst`, `min`, `max` |
| **Primitive streams** | `IntStream`, `LongStream`, `DoubleStream` |
| **Parallel streams** | `parallelStream()` |
| **Grouping & partitioning** | `Collectors.groupingBy` |
| **Optional handling** | `findFirst` + `ifPresentOrElse` |

Feel free to copy the code into an IDE or a file named `StreamDemo.java`, compile it (`javac StreamDemo.java`) and run (`java StreamDemo`). The output demonstrates the power of streams while staying concise.
